From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Erik Bourget <erik@ebourget.net>
Date: Tue, 10 Nov 2026 00:01:24 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] thunderbolt: apple: power-cycle the J713 host router
 for a deep sleep

With thunderbolt_apple.cio_s2r=1 on the J713, the NHI owns the whole
power cycle of its ACIO block around a deep sleep with a dock attached.

In prepare it closes the connection manager, checks that only the
control rings are in use, waits for the released displays' routes to go
down and opens the PHY, DART and tunneled PCIe transactions. In its
noirq suspend, once the PCIe port reports stopped, it puts the routers
to sleep, powers the NHI DART down, powers the controller off, holds
the LSTX pin, takes the four ACIO power domains down in order and
powers the PHY down. The noirq resume does the reverse: PHY, domains,
a cold restart of the controller, tunables, the DART, then the
connection manager's wake. Complete closes the three transactions and
reopens the connection manager.

The host router is told to sleep the way its firmware expects: the
router's own sleep bit (ROUTER_CS_5.SLP, with its PCIe and USB wake
bits clear) first, then the vendor enter-sleep request, then a wait for
the controller to report its sleep state. The vendor request on its own
makes the firmware assert.

After the restart the router is given its lane adapters unlocked and
the cable word again, as at the first start, which is what starts link
training.

A dock lost during the sleep is retired as an unplug once the host side
is back; if the host's PCIe tunnel went with it, the tunneled port is
told not to wait for its link.

A failure after the routers were changed recovers through a restart of
the controller: the router only wakes from its vendor sleep that way.
The ACIO reset cannot be asserted again, so the four domains are taken
down first if they were not already, which resets the CPU, then the
same power-on path runs. If a domain stays on, or the NHI DART could not
be saved before the restart, or the recovery fails, the PCIe and USB
devices behind the dock cannot be resumed safely, so it panics. A DART
that is briefly busy with a mapping is waited for first.

The power-on path replays the T8132 ACIO start sequence the driver
already has for a cable connect.

The parameter defaults to off. Without the PHY reference or the DART,
the NHI logs it and runs without its deep-sleep transaction. Add
host-side models of the ownership rules and of the dock's PM callback
ordering.

This builds on work by Diljit Singh.

